@node gpui-px Manual
@chapter Crate @code{gpui-px}
@cindex gpui-px

@code{gpui-px} is a high-level charting API in the spirit of
Plotly Express, built on top of @ref{gpui-d3rs Manual}. It is the
front-door for "I just want a chart" use cases inside SotF and ships
on crates.io.

@menu
* gpui-px User Guide::
* gpui-px Developer Guide::
@end menu

@node gpui-px User Guide
@section User Guide

In the desktop player, @code{gpui-px} is the layer that turns a slice
of points into a chart with axes, ticks and a colour scale, without
boilerplate.

Six chart types:

@itemize @bullet
@item Scatter
@item Line
@item Bar
@item Heatmap
@item Contour
@item Isoline
@end itemize

Colour scales: Viridis, Plasma, Inferno, Magma, Heat, Coolwarm, Greys
or custom. Both axes support log scaling for multi-magnitude data.

@node gpui-px Developer Guide
@section Developer Guide

@subheading Fluent API

@example
use gpui_px::scatter;

let chart = scatter(&data)
    .x_log()
    .colour_scale(ColorScale::Viridis)
    .title("Frequency response")
    .x_label("Hz")
    .y_label("dB");
@end example

@subheading Validation

Every constructor validates input data (non-empty, finite values,
monotonic axes where required) and returns a typed error. There is no
implicit fallback to garbage output.

@subheading Layer below

@code{gpui-px} owns the high-level builders; @ref{gpui-d3rs Manual}
owns scales, shapes and colours. If you need something more bespoke,
drop down to @code{gpui-d3rs} directly.

@subheading Testing

@example
cargo test -p gpui-px
@end example
